 { The previous chapter has shown the importance of patterns and how to internalise them, regardless of which phase of the game we are talking about. So, to improve our endgame calculation, we need to study patterns that are unique to endgames. And there is no better place to start than theoretical endings. This will be the focus of the chapter. To be clear, this chapter is not a substitute for endgame manuals. Rather, it is a Quickstarter Guide designed to improve your efficiency in studying these manuals. In particular, I will show how variations of a given theoretical endgame will often transpose to other theoretical endgames. This means that instead of memorising moves blindly, we should aim to observe recurring patterns in the endgame and understand the underlying concepts. Comprising 60 positions, this chapter offers a few important tips which will help you navigate confidently even if you are outside your repertoire of endgames. Examples of these tips include “If the defender’s King is in front of the attacker’s pawn, drawing chances significantly increase” and “There must be at least three files/ranks between a checking Rook and the enemy pawn for perpetual check”. As we go along, I will be making references to previous positions. For your easy reference, I recommend that you open a second tab. In this way, you can conveniently compare the current positions we are studying and the referenced positions covered earlier. When going through this chapter, make a conscious effort to identify similar patterns across different endgames. From these patterns, try to infer general principles you believe will improve your overall sense of chess endgames. With regular practice, you will not only better appreciate the positions we will cover, but also develop a deeper understanding of any new positions you study in the future. Don’t worry if you are unable to draw any principles from this chapter because I will list them at the end. On the flip side, don’t be too concerned if your list does not match mine. It is entirely possible to derive other principles from the positions. Before we get started, I’d like to clarify a few terms so that I can write more freely. 1 Rook pawns refer to pawns on the a or h-files 2 Knight pawns refer to pawns on the b or g-files 3 Bishop pawns refer to pawns on the c or f-files 4 Central pawns refer to pawns on the d or e-files 5 The “Attacker” refers to the stronger side, or the side playing for a win 6 The “Defender” refers to the weaker side, or the side playing for a draw } 1. -- 
