 { In a King + pawn vs King endgame, the defending side can draw if the attacker's King is beside or behind its pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf3 f4 { As the defender, your strategy is simple, there are only 3 points to remember: 1. Always aim to stay directly in front of the pawn. In these endgames, that is ALWAYS the best square. 2. If you are forced to retreat, do so along the same file as the pawn. Here, our King is optimally placed on f3. Since we must retreat here, we will move to f2, remaining on the f-file. 3. Take the opposition when the opponent’s king moves to the side of the pawn. } 2. Kf2 Ke4 { Now that the Black King is beside the pawn, we should take the opposition. } 3. Ke2 f3+ 4. Kf2 Kf4 { We must retreat, so let's move to f1 } 5. Kf1 ( 5. Ke1 $4 Ke3 6. Kf1 f2 7. Kg2 Ke2 ) 
 ( 5. Kg1 $4 Kg3 6. Kf1 f2 7. Ke2 Kg2 ) 
Kg3 { We must take the opposition , so } 6. Kg1 Kg4 7. Kf2 Kf4 8. Kf1 Ke4 { Here, we should move to the best square, f2 } 9. Kf2 ( 9. Ke1 $4 Ke3 10. Kf1 f2 11. Kg2 Ke2 ) 
Kf4 { We must retreat here, so we should move to f1 } 10. Kf1 Ke3 11. Ke1 f2+ { What else is there to try? } 12. Kf1 Kf3 { Stalemate is the best Black can do here } 
