 { Let's take a look at the winning technique. This is only possible if our King is in front of our pawn while the opponent does not have the opposition. That is why we should avoid moving our pawn at the start, and instead, advance our King } 1. { -KEY- } Ke2 Kf7 2. Kf3 Kf6 { This plan of advancing our King has been a success. Our King is in front of our pawn and we took the opposition. } 3. Kf4 Ke6 4. Kg5 { Remember, we are the attacker here, so we don't want a draw. Instead of taking the opposition meaninglessly by shadowing the enemy King indefinitely, we should use our King to shove him away. } Kf7 ( 4... Ke5 5. f4 Ke6 6. Kg6 Ke7 7. f5 Kf8 ( 7... Ke8 8. Kg7 { f6-f7-f8 is unstoppable } ) 
8. Kf6 { Our King is in front of our pawn and we took the opposition, so we are winning } Kg8 9. Ke7 { Again, f6-f7-f8 is unstoppable } ) 
5. Kf5 Kg7 { Our King has gained quite a lot of space. It is time to move our pawn up, but how many squares should it advance? 1 or 2? } 6. f3 ( 6. f4 $4 { Would be a big-time blunder since Black can take the opposition, drawing the game (Refer to Position 3) . } Kf7 ) 
Kf7 { Black has still taken the opposition, but we still have a spare pawn move. We can use it to take the opposition } 7. f4 { Even though this isn't a King move, this does take the opposition. We have taken away all the advancing squares of the enemy King. } Ke7 8. Kg6 { Again, we want to fight for more space rather than maintain the opposition. } Ke6 9. f5+ Ke7 10. Kg7 { Once we have our pawn on the fifth rank and our King controlling the rest of the advancing squares of the pawn (f6, f7, and f8), we are guaranteed to score a touchdown. } Ke8 11. f6 Kd7 12. f7 
