 { An attacker's King in front of his pawn can be neutralised with opposition. So before it happens, we should anticipate the King's advance to those squares and prepare to take the opposition. Here, Black will play either Ke4 or Kf4. We want to be able to meet those moves with Ke2 and Kf2 respectively. Hence, we remain on the non-committal square f1, keeping both options of Ke2 and Kf2 open. This is known as distant opposition. } 1. { -KEY- } Kf1 Kf4 ( 1... Ke4 2. Ke2 ) 
 ( 1... Kd4 { By moving to d4, Black threatens to move to e3, where it would have gained too much space. 1…Kd4 also threatens 2…Ke4, which we must meet with 3. Ke2, taking the opposition. Hence, our mission is to stop 2…Ke3 without playing 2. Ke2 and we arrive at the answer, 2. Kf2. } 2. Kf2 ) 
2. Kf2 { And we have already learnt how to draw this position - just follow the Black King (Refer to Position 3) } Kg4 3. Kg2 Kf4 4. Kf2 
