 { The rook pawn has several unique properties, making its rook ending more complex than those of other pawns. For one, the defender has the option to trade Rooks into a drawn King and pawn ending in many more positions (Refer to Position 7 and 8) . Additionally, when the attacker's King moves in front of his pawn, the enemy Rook can sit on the adjacent file, trapping the king. In fact, in this position, despite being cut off by three files, White can hold the draw very comfortably. He just needs to ensure that his King controls the e1 square for as long as the Black Rook remains on the e-file. } 1. { -KEY- } Kd1 Re6 2. Kd2 Re7 3. Kd1 Ra7 4. Ke2 Ra1 5. Kf2 { As it can be seen from this move, Black is one move too slow. He needs to cut the King off by four files to win. Even then, the win involves many lengthy variations which we will analyse next (Position 25). } Rg1 6. Ra8 Rg2+ 7. Kf1 Rg1+ 8. Kf2 Rg6 9. Ra1+ Rg1 10. Rf1 $1 { Immediately forcing a draw by stalemate } Rxf1+ { Black has no other legal moves } 11. Kxf1 
