 { For mating ideas to work, we need the Black King on either h4 or h6. And to force the King to move from h5 to either of those squares, we need to triangulate our Rook with Rg1-g2-g7-g1 } 1. { -KEY- } Rg2 { Black can only move the King. The Black Rook is paralysed on e8 because it needs to stay on the e-file and remain three squares from the pawn. } Kh4 ( 1... Re7 { allows White's King to advance } 2. Kd4 Rd7 3. Ke5 Re7 4. Kd5 Rd7 5. Ke6 { The Black Rook is too close to the White pawn } ) 
 ( 1... Ra8 { allows the pawn to advance } 2. e5 ) 
 ( 1... Kh6 { This allows mating ideas to appear. White must first bring his King to f6 } 2. Kf4 Rf8 3. Ke5 Re8 4. Kf6 $1 { The pawn cannot be taken because of mate } Rxe4 ( 4... Rf8 5. Ke7 { followed by e5 and e6 } ) 
5. Rh2 Rh4 6. Rxh4 ) 
2. Rg7 ( 2. e5 $4 Rxe5 3. Kf4 { Doesn't quite work because White's Rook is on g2 } Kh3 { Counterattacking the Rook and stopping mate } ) 
Kh5 3. Rg1 { The Rooks has triangulated and Black must now move his King to h4 or h6 } ( 3. Kf4 $2 { Building a bridge like in the Lucena position doesn't work } Rf8 4. Ke5 Re8 5. Kf5 Rf8 6. Ke6 Re8 7. Re7 $4 Rg8 $1 { The point! Now that Black has gained control over the g-file, the Black King can inch towards the White pawn and draw the game } ) 
Ra8 ( 3... Kh4 4. e5 Rxe5 5. Kf4 { Now that our Rook is on g1 rather than g2, Black cannot escape mate while counterattacking our Rook. } ) 
 ( 3... Kh6 4. Kf4 Rf8 5. Ke5 Re8 6. Kf6 { With the same mating ideas as we saw in the line 1...Kh6 } ) 
4. e5 Ra4 5. e6 Kh6 ( 5... Ra6 6. Kf4 Rxe6 7. Kf5 { Threatening Black's Rook and Rh1# } ) 
6. e7 Ra8 7. Ke4 Re8 8. Kf5 Rxe7 9. Kf6 { With the same mating idea } 
