 { We move on the final type of Rook endgame that I'd like to discuss: Rook + rook pawn vs Rook where the attacker’s Rook is in front of the pawn, while the defender's Rook is behind the pawn. There is one very important concept you need to understand, and that is the 'safe zone' for the defender's King. This position is a draw because the White Rook on a8 stops its own pawn from promoting and the Rook cannot leave a8 because the a7 pawn will hang. For Black to ensure that this drawing idea continues to hold, the Black King should stay only on g7 or h7. The following variations covering Black's alternate first moves will illustrate what happens if the King leaves this 'safe zone'. } 1. { -KEY- } Kg1 Ra1+ ( 1... Kg6 2. Rg8 { If the Black King moves to the sixth rank, the White Rook will safely give a check and promote next. Black will not have time to play ...Rxa7 If the Black King moves towards the queenside, White has a neat idea } ( 1... Kf7 2. Rh8 { threatening to promote the pawn. Black must take it. } Rxa7 3. Rh7 { With a deadly skewer } ) 
 ( 1... Kh7 { This move is perfectly fine. The King remains in the 'safe zone' } ) 
 ) 
2. Kf2 Ra2+ 3. Ke3 Ra4 4. Kd3 Ra1 5. Kc4 Ra2 6. Kb5 Ra1 7. Kb6 { Now that the White pawn is overprotected, he does threaten to move the Rook away and queen next. For Black to hold the draw, he must give some checks. } Rb1+ 8. Kc7 Rc1+ 9. Kd7 Ra1 10. Kc7 Ra2 11. Kb7 Rb2+ 12. Kc7 { Draw } 
