 { Opposite coloured Bishop endgames have very high drawing tendencies, and this position illustrates that clearly.Even though Black is up three pawns here, the opposite coloured Bishops and the poor pawn position makes this a draw. This is because Black cannot push his pawns any further. White has 2 pieces controlling the squares in front of the pawns whereas Black can have at most one piece (i.e. his King) controlling those squares. If some of the pawns had been on light squares, they could support one another to make progress. Unfortunately for Black, this is not the case. The only thing White needs to do to hold the draw is keep his own Bishop on the long diagonal. White can even play } 1. { -KEY- } Kh1 $1 { Reminding Black that he cannot go 1...f3. } f3 ( 1... Kf5 2. Ba8 Ke5 3. Bb7 Kd4 4. Ba8 Ke3 5. Bb7 Kf2 { This position is the best Black can get - one Black attacker and two White defenders on g2, not enough to make any decisive progress. } ( { Again } 5... f3 { can be met with } 6. Bxf3 Kxf3 { Stalemate. } ) 
 ) 
2. Bxf3+ Kxf3 { And the game ends in stalemate. } 
