 { Even though the attacker had a three pawn material lead in the previous position (Position 49) , the defender still managed to hold the draw. The saving grace was the misplaced pawns on the opposite colour complex to the defender's Bishop. If even just one of those pawns had been on the correct colour complex, the defender would have lost the game. In this position, Black has merely two pawns but they are optimally placed side by side. This not only creates a wall to keep the White King out, but they also remain flexible in how they should be pushed. As the defender, White's first question to answer is: Which critical square do we need to control? } 1. { -KEY- } Be2 { The answer is d3. Black's immediate threat was to play ...d3, followed by ...Kd4, ...c3. By playing 1. Be2, we further control d3 and can meet 1...d3 with 2. Bxd3. Playing 1. Bg6 to control d3 does not work. We will find out why on the next page. } Bb4+ 2. Kc2 Kc5 3. Bf1 Kd5 4. Be2 { White only needs to wait with Be2 and Bf1. The point is that Black cannot ever play ...c3 (because that puts all his pawns on the same colour as his Bishop) or ...d3. The Black King is also unable to make progress as he is tied down to the defence of the c4 pawn. } Ke4 5. Bxc4 
