 { Let's analyse this ending where the defender's King cannot go in front of the pawn. } 1. { -KEY- } Bh6 Ke4 2. Kg2 { First, recognise that a lone Bishop is no match against a King, Bishop and pawn. Passive play, as seen in the 2. Bd2 variation, leads to a loss. Instead, as the Black King rushes to c2, the White King must, likewise, make a run for the c4 square. } ( 2. Bd2 Kd5 3. Ba5 Kc4 4. Bd2 Kb3 5. Ba5 Kc2 6. Bb4 Bf6 7. Ke1 Bc3 8. Bxc3 Kxc3 9. Kd1 d2 10. Ke2 Kc2 ) 
Kd4 3. Kf3 Kc3 4. Ke4 Kc2 5. Kd5 Be1 6. Kc4 { By taking opposition from the rear, Black will never be able to play ...Bc3 to challenge White’s Bishop on the a5-e1 diagonal. } Bd2 7. Bf8 Bf4 8. Bb4 Be5 { If the White King had been far away, the threat of 9...Bc3 would be decisive. Thankfully for White, he's got that under control. } 9. Ba5 Bf4 10. Bc3 Bd2 { Chasing the White Bishop from the a5-e1 diagonal by playing Bd2 is ineffective because it blocks the pawn. } 11. Bg7 Ba5 { If the White Bishop makes way for ...d2 via the a5-e1 diagonal, we will use the c1-h6 diagonal. } ( 11... Bg5 { If the Black Bishop makes way for ...d2 via the c1-h6 diagonal, we will use the a5-e1 diagonal. } ) 
12. Bh6 { To sum this idea up, if the defender's King cannot reach the front of the pawn, he should try to stay behind the enemy King instead. } 
