 { Let's briefly take a look at the single Rook checkmating technique. I included this position certainly not to teach the Rook checkmate, but to demonstrate how the attacker's King chases the defender's. This will give you a deeper appreciation of the two Bishop checkmate. } 1. { -KEY- } Rg6 { As the attacker, we do NOT want to take opposition. Instead, we want Black to take the opposition with 1. ...Ke7, so that we can play Rg7+, pushing him to the last rank. } Kc7 { Black does not want to play ...Ke7 and get forced back, nor does he want to voluntarily walk to the last rank. This move puts up the most resistance. } ( 1... Ke7 2. Rg7 ) 
