 { Before we explore the position, there are three ideas to remember: 1. Checkmate can only be forced in a corner of the same colour complex as your Bishop. For example, if you have a dark-squared Bishop, you can only force checkmate at a dark-squared corner. 2. Waiting moves are sometimes necessary, and should only be done by the King or the Bishop, never by the Knight. 3. The Bishop and Knight will coordinate best when the Knight is on a square of the same colour complex as the Bishop. For example, if you have a light-squared Bishop, the Knight is best placed on a light square. Now that you know the general ideas, let's check out the specific technique. Step 1: Control the centre } 1. { -KEY- } Bd3 Kd4 2. Ke2 Ke5 3. Ke3 Kd5 4. Be4+ Kc5 5. Kd3 Kb4 6. Kd4 Kb3 { Black cannot keep his King in the middle now that White is occupying the centre. The next best option is run to the dark squared corner where mate cannot be forced } 7. Ne2 Ka2 8. Kc3 Ka1 9. Nd4 Ka2 10. Nc2 { The Knight is the only piece that can force the king out of a1. } Kb1 11. Kb3 Kc1 12. Nd4 Kd2 { Notice how the last three moves by White have pushed the enemy King to the checkmating corner. } 13. Ne6 Ke3 14. Bg6 { Thanks to the Bishop and Knight both working on light squares, White has achieved perfect coordination. The White Knight, Bishop, and King work together to form a prison of 17 squares } Kd2 15. Kb2 Kd1 16. Bd3 { White wants to play Bc2 only after ...Kd2 to force the enemy back, hence this waiting move } Kd2 17. Bc2 Ke2 18. Kc1 Ke3 19. Kd1 Kf3 20. Kd2 Kg4 21. Bg6 { Covering h5 } Kf3 22. Kd3 Kg4 { Other moves allow Bh5 } 23. Ke3 Kg3 24. Bh5 Kh4 25. Be2 { Now that the Bishop has improved its position, the Knight must follow suit } Kg3 26. Ng5 Kh4 27. Ne4 { The Knight and the Bishop alone trap the King in a prison of 6 squares. The White King is free to further restrict the movement of the enemy. } Kh3 28. Kf4 Kh4 29. Kf5 Kh3 30. Kg5 Kg2 31. Kh4 Kh2 32. Bf1 Kg1 33. Bh3 Kh2 34. Nc3 Kg1 35. Kg3 { Similar to what we saw in the two Bishop checkmate, the King and light-squared Bishop alone trap the enemy King in a prison of two squares, g1 and h1. White will now take both squares away, one at a time. } Kh1 36. Bg2+ { Taking away h1 } Kg1 37. Ne2# { Taking away g1 with mate } 
