 { Here are some of the general principles from the 60 positions we can infer. 1. As the attacker, you’d much rather have a central pawn than a flank pawn. This is often true whether the endgames involves Rooks, Queens, or even no pieces. One of the few exceptions is when the attacker has a rook pawn and a King vs a lone Knight (Refer to Position 47) . 2. If the defender’s King is in front of the attacker’s pawn, drawing chances significantly increase. Again, this can be observed whether in endgames with Rooks, Queens, Bishops, or in pure pawn endgames. The reason is simple. The defender has the option to trade down to a drawn pawn endgame. Refer to Positions 2, 3, 5, 21, 22, 37, and 53 3. There must be at least THREE files/ranks between a checking Rook and the enemy pawn for perpetual check. I am sure you’ve seen this one many times throughout this chapter (Refer to Positions 20, 23, 24, 26, 28, 31 and 32) . 4. In opposite coloured Bishop endings, if the attacker’s pawns are on the opposite colour complex to the defender’s Bishop, drawing chances greatly increase. (Refer to position 49) 5. In same coloured Bishop endings where the attacker has a single extra pawn, if the defender cannot bring his King in front of the pawn, his best chance is to take opposition from the rear. (Refer to Position 54) 6. Knights perform best in the centre where they have the most options. The Knight’s lack of space really shows when it is pit against a King and a pawn on the seventh rank. Against central pawns, the Knight has more space and is able to draw (Refer to Position 45) . However, against rook pawns, the Knight is cramped and cannot hold the fort (Refer to Position 47) . Unsurprisingly, the only exception to this is if the rook pawn were on the sixth rank (or closer to the centre) , where the Knight has ample room to fend off the King and the pawn (Refer to Position 48) . 7. The Bishop and Knight will coordinate best when the Knight is on a square of the same colour complex as the Bishop. This coordination is evident in the Bishop and Knight checkmate (Refer to position 60) . } 1. -- 
